  "notes": "{羽化|うか} is the final stage of insect metamorphosis: when an adult insect emerges from its pupa or final nymph stage and unfolds its wings.\n\nUSAGE:\n- Composed of {羽|う} (wing) + {化|か} (transformation); literally, 'becoming winged.'\n- Functions as a する verb and as a noun.\n- Used primarily in biology, entomology, and nature writing.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {蝶|ちょう}が{羽化|うか}する: a butterfly emerges from its pupa\n- {セミの羽化|うか}: the emergence of a cicada (a popular subject in summer nature observation)\n- {羽化|うか}{直後|ちょくご}: just after emergence\n- {羽化|うか}{不全|ふぜん}: incomplete eclosion (when an insect fails to emerge properly)\n\nCULTURAL NOTE:\n- Observing insect {羽化|うか} is a classic Japanese elementary school science activity; many children raise butterflies, beetles, or cicadas to watch the process.\n- The Daoist expression {羽化|うか}{登仙|とうせん} ('becoming winged and ascending to the immortals') uses the same characters figuratively, evoking the idea of a person reaching a transcendent state.",
